    <nmo:Mint rdf:about="http://nomisma.org/id/thorn_nl">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://nomisma.org/id/"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="nl">Thorn</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Thorn</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="de">Thorn</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Thorn, a municipality in the Dutch province of Limburg, developed around a Benedictine convent founded in 992 and closed in 1797. As early as the beginning of the 11th century, Abbess Gerberga had coins minted in her own name. Regular coinage only began in the 16th century during the abbesseships of Margaretha van Brederode (1531–1577) and Anna van der Marck (1604–1631). These were mostly imitations of well-known examples, sometimes original types, and often of inferior quality, which led to difficulties. In 1561, Emperor Ferdinand banned coinage in Thorn; Margaretha van Brederode was summoned before the Chamber Court in Spier, but coinage resumed in 1562. Her successor, Josina van der Marck (1577–1604), was, however, not permitted to mint coins. Her sister and successor, Anna van der Marck (1604–1631), resumed coinage in Thorn, though this was subject to strict restrictions imposed by the Westphalian League. It is likely that no more gold was minted under her, and, apart from a few trial pieces for double daalders, only shillings and smaller denominations were struck. The coat of arms of Margaretha van Brederode features four red lions on a silver field. That of Anna van der Marck is quartered with Marck, Wassenaar-Leiden, Runkel and Lumey, with a heart shield bearing Schoonhoven.</skos:definition>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://wiki.muntenenpapiergeld.nl/index.php?title=Muntplaatsen"/>
        <geo:location rdf:resource="http://nomisma.org/id/thorn_nl#this"/>
